{"text": "DRAFT 2 -- OAC edits in bold -- not for filing\nIN THE UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT FOR THE NORTHERN DISTRICT OF OHIO, EASTERN DIVISION\nPARIS DELACROIX-MBEKI, Plaintiff,\nv.\nSTAVROS PRECISION HYDRAULICS, INC., and HUNTER J. STAVROS, Defendants.\nCase No. 1:26-cv-00832-PAB\nJudge Marguerite T. Vance\nPLAINTIFF'S MOTION TO COMPEL DISCOVERY RESPONSES AND FOR FEES\nPlaintiff Paris Delacroix-Mbeki moves under Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(3)(B) and Local Civil Rule 7.1 for an order compelling Defendants Stavros Precision Hydraulics, Inc. (\"SPH\") and Hunter J. Stavros to answer Interrogatory No. 9 and to produce the documents called for by Request for Production No. 14.\nBackground\nMs. Delacroix-Mbeki was SPH's controller from March 4, 2019 until she was fired on November 21, 2025, nine days after she reported to the board that Mr. Stavros had charged roughly $212,000 in personal expenses to the company's corporate card. She alleges retaliation and unpaid bonus compensation of $48,750.00. Defendants answered that she resigned and that she deleted company files on her last day.\nWhether she deleted anything turns on SPH's own access logs. SPH's outside IT vendor, Ironbridge Managed IT, LLC (\"Ironbridge\"), has admitted in a letter (Exhibit D) that the logs exist. According to that letter the finance share was accessed at 11:52 p.m. on the night of 11/21/2025 from IP address 203.0.113.47, using the administrator account hstavros_adm, and not from the address assigned to Plaintiff's home connection, 198.51.100.212. The same letter identifies the machine as a laptop with serial number PF-3XK8W21 issued to Mr. Stavros, not the laptop Plaintiff returned (serial PF-2RM4T90).\nThe requests and the responses\nPlaintiff served her first set of requests on June 12, 2026. Request for Production No. 14 asks for \"all access, VPN and authentication logs for the finance file share for November 2025.\" Interrogatory No. 9 asks Defendants to identify every person who used the hstavros_adm account that month. Responses were due 30 days later. Defendants served objections only, on July 20, 2026, calling the requests \"overbroad\" and \"not proportional.\"\nCounsel conferred by telephone on August 6, 2026 and again by video on Aug. 19. Defense counsel, Bartholomew Quigley of Quigley Forsythe Denham, P.C., said the logs would be produced \"once the client finds them.\" On September 1, 2026 his partner Leland Forsythe wrote that SPH now believes the logs were overwritten. Ironbridge's letter says otherwise. Declaration of Irene Castaneda (Ironbridge's service manager) is being requested; Castaneda says she will sign only under subpoena.\nArgument\nThe logs are relevant under Rule 26(b)(1) because they go to the only defense pleaded. They are proportional because they cover one share for one month and already sit on a vendor portal, https://portal.ironbridge-it.example/tickets/88214, under ticket #88214. Defendants have never said what the burden would be. An unexplained boilerplate objection is no objection at all.\nSeun -- add the cases from the Hartwell brief here. Also decide whether we mention that Hunter told the board Paris was \"unstable\" because she had taken leave for a depressive episode in 2024. I would leave it out of this motion. -- OAC\nRelief requested\nPlaintiff asks the Court to (1) compel complete responses within 14 days; (2) order SPH to instruct Ironbridge to preserve and produce the logs; and (3) award fees of $6,340 (15.1 hours) under Rule 37(a)(5)(A).\nRespectfully submitted,\n/s/ Oluwaseun Adeyemi-Clark\nOluwaseun Adeyemi-Clark (Ohio Bar No. 0091764)\nAdeyemi-Clark & Rooney LLP\n1375 East Ninth Street, 21st Floor\nCleveland, Ohio 44114-1724\n216.555.0177 | fax 216.555.0178\noac@acr-law.example\nCertificate of service\nI certify that on September 14, 2026 a copy of this motion was served by e-mail on Bartholomew Quigley, bquigley@qfdlaw.example, and Leland Forsythe, lforsythe@qfdlaw.example, counsel for Defendants.", "kin